NNAAMMEE
       uselib - load shared library

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ##iinncclluuddee <<uunniissttdd..hh>>

       [[[[ddeepprreeccaatteedd]]]] iinntt uusseelliibb((ccoonnsstt cchhaarr **_l_i_b_r_a_r_y));;

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  system call uusseelliibb() serves to load a shared library to be used by
       the calling process.  It is given a pathname.   The  address  where  to
       load  is  found in the library itself.  The library can have any recog‐
       nized binary format.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       On success, zero is returned.  On error, -1 is returned, and  _e_r_r_n_o  is
       set to indicate the error.

EERRRROORRSS
       In  addition to all of the error codes returned by ooppeenn(2) and mmmmaapp(2),
       the following may also be returned:

       EEAACCCCEESS The library specified by _l_i_b_r_a_r_y does not have read  or  execute
              permission,  or  the  caller does not have search permission for
              one of the directories in the path prefix.  (See also ppaatthh__rreessoo‐‐
              lluuttiioonn(7).)

       EENNFFIILLEE The system‐wide limit on the total number of open files has been
              reached.

       EENNOOEEXXEECC
              The file specified by _l_i_b_r_a_r_y is not an executable  of  a  known
              type; for example, it does not have the correct magic numbers.

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
       Linux.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
       This obsolete system call is not supported by glibc.  No declaration is
       provided  in  glibc headers, but, through a quirk of history, glibc be‐
       fore glibc 2.23 did export an ABI for this system call.  Therefore,  in
       order to employ this system call, it was sufficient to manually declare
       the  interface in your code; alternatively, you could invoke the system
       call using ssyyssccaallll(2).

       In ancient libc versions (before glibc 2.0), uusseelliibb() was used to  load
       the  shared  libraries with names found in an array of names in the bi‐
       nary.

       Since Linux 3.15, this system call is available only when the kernel is
       configured with the CCOONNFFIIGG__UUSSEELLIIBB option.

